{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible","id":1471534,"title":"Sorcery: a surrogate for the Yearning is possible?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-12-31T08:49:08-08:00","id":1471534,"title":"Sorcery: a surrogate for the Yearning is possible?","content":"According to the Whitewolf fandom wiki:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#3A3A3A\"><span style=\"font-family:rubik\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">In contrast to </span></span></span><a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Psychic_(WOD)\" target=\"_blank\">psychic abilities</a><span style=\"color:#3A3A3A\"><span style=\"font-family:rubik\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, sorcery is not an innate ability and has to be learned. As such, it is always a deliberate choice, instead of an accident. Individuals that have taken up the study of sorcery describe themselves driven by something they refer to as the </span></span></span><i>Yearning</i><span style=\"color:#3A3A3A\"><span style=\"font-family:rubik\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">. This yearning manifests as a hunger for occult secrets and hidden knowledge that every sorcerer shares. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> Here's the question: a person who don't feel the innate hunger for secrets and knowledge but is strongly motivated by:<br /> <br /> <br /> - obsession about his own ineluctable mortality coupled by the will to extend his life by any possible means<br /> - desire for expediency and for a &quot;shortcut&quot; that would put him a step above the normal human beings<br /> - fear, helpnlessnes and frustration at his condition of common mortal<br /> - anger and resentment at real or perceived wrongs<br /> - base greed and lust for power<br /> - envy toward supernatural creature's might<br /> - narcissistic love for the self and drive to impose that feeling upon his fellows<br /> - a bit of almost scientific curiosity<br /> <br /> Could ALL these motivations put together be enough to compensate a lack of innate Yearning?<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27051-solomon-draak","name":"Solomon Draak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-31T08:57:19-08:00","id":1471535,"title":"","content":"Yearning is a narrative thing, not a factual, in-setting thing. And every sorcerer <i>is</i> going to manifest the Yearning before he becomes a sorcerer; but there's some question as to the order of events: do you start with a Yearning, and then demonstrate some of all of the motivations you describe? Or do you start with those motivations and then develop a Yearning? It could go either way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-31T10:17:45-08:00","id":1471541,"title":"","content":"This is more a personal interpretation/opinion, but I would also draw a distinction between a &quot;sorcerer&quot; and &quot;someone who has learned some sorcery.&quot; For example, I would not be surprised if certain groups (hunters, demon-worshippers, etc.) might teach certain bits of sorcery to their members. In those cases, the sorcery learned will reflect the agenda of the organization or its members, but their knowledge will mostly never expand beyond those specific Paths that serve their immediate goals, because sorcery is just a means, not an end. In contrast, for a true Sorcerer, sorcery is an end unto itself. A true Sorcerer will seek out new Paths, new magical secrets, without requiring any external circumstances to motivate them, actively driven by the hunger for occult secrets, aka the Yearning.<br /> <br /> Since it's hot pop-culture right now, and since I just bought the game for super-cheap on Steam, lol, a good example might be Witchers. They learn a few bits of magic, powerful magic even, but never more than what they can use for their work as monster-hunters. In contrast, Mages dig into the secrets of magic, expanding knowledge in the field through investigation.<br /> <br /> PS The particular motivations are quite relevant, so I'll address the examples you've provided:<br /> <br /> Fear of mortality and danger, greed and lust for power, anger, etc., are good examples of motivators that might push a person to learn some sorcery without being a sorcerer. But they have built-in limits regarding what sorcery would actually interest the individual: a person driven by anger isn't likely to learn healing of protective arts, someone driven by greed probably isn't going to seek out knowledge with intangible benefits. Lust for power is probably the closest to a universal motivation, but even that is going to push you to favour certain Paths over others.<br /> <br /> &quot;Scientific curiosity&quot; kind of borders on distinction-without-a-difference to me; it's still a Yearning, but the person involved is likely to end up an Extraordinary Citizen in the Technocracy, acquiring &quot;techno-sorcery&quot; Paths rather than the more traditional (or Traditional, heh) mystic Paths.<br /> <br /> Laziness and shortcut-seeking is the one example I think I would rule against. If sorcery was that easy and safe, everyone would be learning it. Even with the Technocracy actively trying to suppress it, it would be pretty hard to keep people from passing that knowledge around if any slacker could learn to fly because walking is too much of a bother.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","user":"Kharnov","date":". 12-31-2021, 01:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-31T11:01:48-08:00","id":1471543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kharnov</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible?p=1471541#post1471541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Laziness and shortcut-seeking is the one example I think I would rule against. If sorcery was that easy and safe, everyone would be learning it. Even with the Technocracy actively trying to suppress it, it would be pretty hard to keep people from passing that knowledge around if any slacker could learn to fly because walking is too much of a bother.</div> </div> </div> </div>I must clarify this point.<br /> <br /> Desire for expedience and shortcut-seeking is not <i>necessarily</i> the same thing as laziness. It can be, but not always.<br /> <br /> The point is that a person can study, apply himself and struggles for 20 years, and still end up with nothing ( or very little ). There are plenty of cultured, disciplined and determined people who get trumped by nepotism, racial or religious prejudice or pure and simple bad luck.<br /> <br /> Sorcery gives the sorcerer an edge that he wouldn't otherwise have, that's undeniable.<br /> <br /> Beside that, short-cut seeking means disposition toward amoral or outright immoral means toward an end, like living sacrifices to spirits, unethical experimentation, or even infernalism. That does not necessarily means the would-be sorcerer is lazy. He could thing that to succed he must both work hard AND cheat, because there are so many rivals who do the same.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27051-solomon-draak","name":"Solomon Draak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-31T11:01:48-08:00","id":1471544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kharnov</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible?p=1471541#post1471541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Laziness and shortcut-seeking is the one example I think I would rule against. If sorcery was that easy and safe, everyone would be learning it. Even with the Technocracy actively trying to suppress it, it would be pretty hard to keep people from passing that knowledge around if any slacker could learn to fly because walking is too much of a bother.</div> </div> </div> </div>I must clarify this point.<br /> <br /> Desire for expedience and shortcut-seeking is not <i>necessarily</i> the same thing as laziness. It can be, but not always.<br /> <br /> The point is that a person can study, apply himself and struggles for 20 years, and still end up with nothing ( or very little ). There are plenty of cultured, disciplined and determined people who get trumped by nepotism, racial or religious prejudice or pure and simple bad luck.<br /> <br /> Sorcery gives the sorcerer an edge that he wouldn't otherwise have, that's undeniable.<br /> <br /> Beside that, short-cut seeking means disposition toward amoral or outright immoral means toward an end, like living sacrifices to spirits, unethical experimentation, or even infernalism. That does not necessarily means the would-be sorcerer is lazy. He could thing that to succed he must both work hard AND cheat, because there are so many rivals who do the same.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27051-solomon-draak","name":"Solomon Draak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-31T11:02:15-08:00","id":1471545,"title":"","content":"The distinction between hedge magic and sorcery is real. In particular, the hedge magic featured in Hunters Hunted 2, Kinfolk, and Ghost Hunters is not the same as the sorcery featured in any of the Sorcerer books. The former three feature dabblers in the arts, who don't have any sort of cohesive theory of magic; just a grab bag of tricks. By contrast, sorcerers are those students of the mystical arts who go above and beyond mere dabbling and <i>do</i> have cohesive frameworks of mystical principles and practices. <br /> <br /> For the record, I'm mainly referring to X20 products here; though given that the X20 Sorcerer book is a Storyteller's Vault product and thus not official, I'm tending to rely more on the original Sorcerer for the lore. Still, it's worth noting that in all iterations of Sorcerer, you have something to paradigms, practices, and tools, whereas the other examples of hedge magic (HH2, Kinfolk, and Ghost Hunters) do nothing of the sort. <br /> <br /> Also bear in mind that there isn't a unified hedge magic system: although Paths in each sourcebook share common names, the underlying game systems are often different. And in one case, something that's a Hedge Path in the other books is instead a Psychic Phenomenon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-31T11:26:33-08:00","id":1471548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A true Sorcerer will seek out new Paths, new magical secrets, without requiring any external circumstances to motivate them, actively driven by the hunger for occult secrets, aka the Yearning. </div> </div> </div>I agree.<br /> But an individual driven by desires and ambition can be relentless in his pursuit of power too.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lust for power is probably the closest to a universal motivation, but even that is going to push you to favour certain Paths over others. </div> </div> </div>In my opinion, almost every kind of occult power ( not mere theorical knowledge; <i>applied </i>knowledge which produces tangible results ) can be used - and abused - for personal gain.<br /> A person driven by resentment which has the opportunity to learn healing magic could easily turn these seeming benevolent gift into a weapon. He could send maladies into remission, then blackmail his patient / victim into doing his bidding. He could create a fanatic cult that reveres him as a sacred healer and send his zealots to perform unspeakable acts.<br /> <br /> Or, more simply, he could become very rich by healing ill millionaires ( or even wealthy middle-classers ), then pay mercenaries / thugs / hackers / internet trolls-for-hire to perform his revenge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27051-solomon-draak","name":"Solomon Draak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-31T12:46:37-08:00","id":1471552,"title":"","content":"I would note that psychologically, sorcerers are identical to mages. Both are inherently willful and driven (with the arguable exception of some religious devotees like the Celestial Chorus, who actively cultivate humility and thus play against type). The only real difference between a Mage and a sorcerer is that a mage has access to Awakened Magick well a sorcerer has access to sorcerous Paths. <br /> <br /> Mind you, more can be made of that distinction. M20 states that the Awakening is a realization that the maids has an innate quality that allows him to change the world; and the hedge wizards handbook says that sorcerers are empowered by a connection to an Otherworld \u2014 which, in more General terms, boils down to making use of existing power in the world around them rather than using an innate quality of their own. I'm leery of reading too much into that, as there are indications of mages who view their power as being from an external source; and it seems quite reasonable that there could be sorcerers who believe that their power comes from something within themselves, in a manner of speaking; so I tend to view that particular distinction as being more of a tendency than an absolute rule.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-31T14:05:39-08:00","id":1471556,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible?p=1471545#post1471545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The distinction between hedge magic and sorcery is real. In particular, the hedge magic featured in Hunters Hunted 2, Kinfolk, and Ghost Hunters is not the same as the sorcery featured in any of the Sorcerer books. The former three feature dabblers in the arts, who don't have any sort of cohesive theory of magic; just a grab bag of tricks. By contrast, sorcerers are those students of the mystical arts who go above and beyond mere dabbling and <i>do</i> have cohesive frameworks of mystical principles and practices.<br /> <br /> For the record, I'm mainly referring to X20 products here; though given that the X20 Sorcerer book is a Storyteller's Vault product and thus not official, I'm tending to rely more on the original Sorcerer for the lore. Still, it's worth noting that in all iterations of Sorcerer, you have something to paradigms, practices, and tools, whereas the other examples of hedge magic (HH2, Kinfolk, and Ghost Hunters) do nothing of the sort.<br /> <br /> Also bear in mind that there isn't a unified hedge magic system: although Paths in each sourcebook share common names, the underlying game systems are often different. And in one case, something that's a Hedge Path in the other books is instead a Psychic Phenomenon.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm willing to acknowledge an error on my part, in that case. I have not actually read into the Sorcerer books specifically; beyond knowing that the Revised Sorcerer added multiple Aspects to the various Paths, I've generally viewed hedge magic and sorcery as synonymous. A shortcoming of using the wiki as a starting point until I have time to dig into a particular topic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solomon Draak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible?p=1471544#post1471544\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I must clarify this point.<br /> <br /> Desire for expedience and shortcut-seeking is not <i>necessarily</i> the same thing as laziness. It can be, but not always.<br /> <br /> The point is that a person can study, apply himself and struggles for 20 years, and still end up with nothing ( or very little ). There are plenty of cultured, disciplined and determined people who get trumped by nepotism, racial or religious prejudice or pure and simple bad luck.<br /> <br /> Sorcery gives the sorcerer an edge that he wouldn't otherwise have, that's undeniable.<br /> <br /> Beside that, short-cut seeking means disposition toward amoral or outright immoral means toward an end, like living sacrifices to spirits, unethical experimentation, or even infernalism. That does not necessarily means the would-be sorcerer is lazy. He could thing that to succed he must both work hard AND cheat, because there are so many rivals who do the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough to distinguish between the two, but I don't know if I would agree that sorcery provides enough of an advantage to justify pursuing it over any other possible routes to power unless you have that pre-existing Yearning for occult knowledge. It seems like it would be a rather specific set of conditions that would make &quot;learn sorcery&quot; the expedient route. Most sorcery Paths just aren't *that* powerful AFAIK that you can't just acquire the mundane skills or equipment and have them cover what you need with less investment of time and effort (aka experience points).<br /> <br /> Put another way, how much can a Sorcerer achieve that can't be pulled off just by getting rich through any number of mundane methods and then hiring someone else to do it? This goes back to Dataweaver's point that there probably *is* some Yearning there too if that's really the route you're going to choose.<br /> <br /> Infernalism is probably the main exception, and the fools that go down that route get what they deserve. That's not expedient, that's *desperate*.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solomon Draak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible?p=1471548#post1471548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree.<br /> But an individual driven by desires and ambition can be relentless in his pursuit of power too.<br /> <br /> In my opinion, almost every kind of occult power ( not mere theorical knowledge; <i>applied </i>knowledge which produces tangible results ) can be used - and abused - for personal gain.<br /> A person driven by resentment which has the opportunity to learn healing magic could easily turn these seeming benevolent gift into a weapon. He could send maladies into remission, then blackmail his patient / victim into doing his bidding. He could create a fanatic cult that reveres him as a sacred healer and send his zealots to perform unspeakable acts.<br /> <br /> Or, more simply, he could become very rich by healing ill millionaires ( or even wealthy middle-classers ), then pay mercenaries / thugs / hackers / internet trolls-for-hire to perform his revenge.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree, which is why I said it would &quot;push&quot; you towards certain Paths, not guarantee or strictly determine which ones you would choose. But if you're primarily motivated by resentment, for example, then I think the more likely impulse will be to directly harm the target of your resentment, rather than the more convoluted plan of becoming a healer in order to find a way to turn that against your target. To quote Scott Evil, &quot;why don't you just shoot them?&quot; I'm not saying the situation doesn't exist, it just doesn't seem like it will generally be the most expedient choice. If you don't already have a pile of occult resources just sitting in front of you, pursuing the more mundane options seems like less of a waste of time to me, if all you really care about is power or convenience.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-01T06:57:49-08:00","id":1471590,"title":"","content":"What you are talking about is <b>hatred</b> , not simple resentment.<br /> I agree, a person motivated by hatred would simply shoot the hated one.<br /> <br /> Resentment is a more complicated animal. It's akin to Envy ( while hatred is the brother of Wrath ). <br /> The resentful wants to shine and put those which he resent under his shadow. He wants to superate and humiliate them.<br /> <br /> A resentful heart is not even necessarily an evil heart ( although it often is ). A good-natured person can still feel the pangs of envy and resentment, although he'll not act to hurt people.<br /> Becoming a ( successful and powerful ) healer would be great for a wounded ego. <br /> <br /> - - -<br /> <br /> Hatred is... more &quot;pure&quot;. More simple. And all-consuming, self-destructive and blind.<br /> The kind of hatred a person feel when his loved ones are killed for no apparent reason behyond mere cruelty.<br /> <br /> While resentment comes from a big ego, hatred comes from a destroyed ego.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27051-solomon-draak","name":"Solomon Draak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-03T21:09:06-08:00","id":1471766,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solomon Draak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible#post1471534\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- obsession about his own ineluctable mortality coupled by the will to extend his life by any possible means<br /> - desire for expediency and for a &quot;shortcut&quot; that would put him a step above the normal human beings<br /> - fear, helpnlessnes and frustration at his condition of common mortal<br /> - anger and resentment at real or perceived wrongs<br /> - base greed and lust for power<br /> - envy toward supernatural creature's might<br /> - narcissistic love for the self and drive to impose that feeling upon his fellows<br /> - a bit of almost scientific curiosity<br /> <br /> Could ALL these motivations put together be enough to compensate a lack of innate Yearning?</div> </div> </div> </div>Lets put it this way:<br /> <br /> Why did this person chose to follow through their motivation through <b>sorcery</b>?<br /> <br /> You can go after those things through any means. To chose sorcery you must:<br /> <br /> 1 - Know it is real;<br /> <br /> 2 - Know how to navigate it;<br /> <br /> 3 - Dedicate yourself tremendously to learn a lot of otherwise useless stuff.<br /> <br /> The Yearning isn't a primary motivation, it is a mindset. The lack of it means that other answers to your plight will be more cost-effective for you <b><u>by far</u></b>.<br /> <br /> Sorcery isn't a shortcut or a means to obtain those any more than dedicating yourself to a PhD before you even finish High School is. You'll start without even knowing what you'll achieve. From a player perspective is just some dots and XP in the sheet, but from a character perspective is the crowning of long years of effort.<br /> <br /> A long journey that, mind you, won't only be fraught with danger and dead ends (you'll end up learning a lot of stuff that don't actually work before you have a chance to start understanding something), it will also point you to simpler means to achieve your goals that leave you with space to actually dedicate yourself to your goals. Like becoming a Ghoul or paying a Maqe to do your biding.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-04T05:32:14-08:00","id":1471773,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The lack of it means that other answers to your plight will be more cost-effective for you <b><u>by far</u></b>. </div> </div> </div>But that is simply not true.<br /> <br /> No amount of self-help courses and psychology study can grant you the magnetism that even a single dot in the Path of Fascination gives.<br /> No amount of mundane medicine can reply even the middle levels of the Path of Healing.<br /> No amount of study about finance and economy can turn a normal person into a billionaire if he hasn't an innate knack for business, while the Path of Divination can.<br /> <br /> If you want to ruin the life of someone, or even killing him, the Path of Cursing allow that with next to zero chance of repercussion. Even if you are rich, hiring a killer is way more riskful and uncertain.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Like becoming a Ghoul </div> </div> </div>Are you kidding me?<br /> Becoming a ghoul means either become a slave to an undead monster who can choose to drain you in every moment because the hunt didn't go well,<br /> or constantly hunt uber-dangerous and impredictable undead monsters to get your fix.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why did this person chose to follow through their motivation through <b>sorcery</b>? </div> </div> </div>Because sorcery, as is described in the WoD, makes you a god between mortals even at the lowest levels.<br /> Training and study in mundane fields gets you something if you are blessed with talent, and even then it's not a guarantee.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27051-solomon-draak","name":"Solomon Draak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-05T06:27:35-08:00","id":1471841,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solomon Draak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible?p=1471773#post1471773\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because sorcery, as is described in the WoD, makes you a god between mortals even at the lowest levels.<br /> Training and study in mundane fields gets you something if you are blessed with talent, and even then it's not a guarantee.</div> </div> </div> </div>All your considerations are valid from the extremely well informed player perspective, but still don't consider the character perspective.<br /> <br /> Most importantly, you're not understanding the core of the <i>Yearning</i>: it is a way to demonstrate that sorcery <b>is itself a field that requires a lot of talent and effort, and even then with no guarantees</b>.<br /> <br /> You as a player can just buy dots in skills and Resources and say your character is a wealthy master of their craft. You as a person knows that actually achieving that, for the average person, isn't so simple. Sorcery is the exact same thing. You're looking at it as a player, that need only spend points to get dots, but for the character those dots are all the same representative of something you must have a helluva lot talent and effort to get. <b>That's what the Yearning represents</b>.<br /> <br /> It is not your motivation, it is the talent to achieve it. If you're a sorcerer proper, this is the same as saying you're one of the most skilled lawyers or doctors around, the kind of person that can command any price for their service. I'm not talking power, I'm talking ow much talent and effort you need to get there.<br /> <br /> So think through all those considerations by this lens. Becoming a sorcerer isn't a substitute for natural magnetism and experience as an actor and seducer any more than becoming a top model or world-renowned actor is a substitute for talent and learning sorcery. This is not just about your motivation to learn, it is about your effort <b>and talent</b>, as you would put it, as talent itself is a very loose term that don't apply to any of those other fields any more than it applies to sorcery.<br /> <br /> When you talk about substituting the <i>Yearning</i> by some motivation, it is the same thing as substituting the talent to be a star on your field by a motivation. But at the point that someone is so obsessed that they can overcome lack of talent to become successful at something to this degree, isn't this obsession a form of talent on itself? If you have enough of an obsession with your motives to pursue sorcery and have success at it, then this obsession is itself the <i>Yearning</i>. You substituted nothing at all.<br /> <br /> Again, keeping in mind that even knowing that it exists, it works, and how it works is already far, far from a given from an IC perspective, and the people that have this information is already either obsessed by the occult or heavily in contact with it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-05T07:53:00-08:00","id":1471843,"title":"","content":"I tought that the Yearning is the kind of love for occult knowledge in itself that would motivate a person to study even without getting anything else from it except the pleasure of knowledge.<br /> A bit like philosophy.<br /> <br /> Final question:<br /> <br /> a surrogate is, by definition, something inferior to the optimal stuff.<br /> <br /> Could various motivations lead a person, if not to learn as much as a Sorcerer, to learn... <i>something</i>, mabye a single dot in a single Path?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27051,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27051-solomon-draak","user":"Solomon Draak","date":". 01-05-2022, 11:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27051-solomon-draak","name":"Solomon Draak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-05T09:48:27-08:00","id":1471848,"title":"","content":"Sure! Take a look at the vampire hunters in hh2. They do not have a Yearning (or, as subsequent books have called it, a calling); but they can and often do acquire dots in Hedge Magic.<br /> <br /> In their case, it's not that they have an affinity for supernatural Mysteries; it said they want tools that they can use to hunt vampires.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 01-05-2022, 12:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-05T10:28:29-08:00","id":1471850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible?p=1471841#post1471841\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All your considerations are valid from the extremely well informed player perspective, but still don't consider the character perspective.<br /> <br /> Most importantly, you're not understanding the core of the <i>Yearning</i>: it is a way to demonstrate that sorcery <b>is itself a field that requires a lot of talent and effort, and even then with no guarantees</b>.<br /> <br /> You as a player can just buy dots in skills and Resources and say your character is a wealthy master of their craft. You as a person knows that actually achieving that, for the average person, isn't so simple. Sorcery is the exact same thing. You're looking at it as a player, that need only spend points to get dots, but for the character those dots are all the same representative of something you must have a helluva lot talent and effort to get. <b>That's what the Yearning represents</b>.<br /> <br /> It is not your motivation, it is the talent to achieve it. If you're a sorcerer proper, this is the same as saying you're one of the most skilled lawyers or doctors around, the kind of person that can command any price for their service. I'm not talking power, I'm talking ow much talent and effort you need to get there.<br /> <br /> So think through all those considerations by this lens. Becoming a sorcerer isn't a substitute for natural magnetism and experience as an actor and seducer any more than becoming a top model or world-renowned actor is a substitute for talent and learning sorcery. This is not just about your motivation to learn, it is about your effort <b>and talent</b>, as you would put it, as talent itself is a very loose term that don't apply to any of those other fields any more than it applies to sorcery.<br /> <br /> When you talk about substituting the <i>Yearning</i> by some motivation, it is the same thing as substituting the talent to be a star on your field by a motivation. But at the point that someone is so obsessed that they can overcome lack of talent to become successful at something to this degree, isn't this obsession a form of talent on itself? If you have enough of an obsession with your motives to pursue sorcery and have success at it, then this obsession is itself the <i>Yearning</i>. You substituted nothing at all.<br /> <br /> Again, keeping in mind that even knowing that it exists, it works, and how it works is already far, far from a given from an IC perspective, and the people that have this information is already either obsessed by the occult or heavily in contact with it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure about this sorcery as talent analogy. I've always read it as a desire to understand esoteric mysteries. On the other hand, I am familiar with the notion that you tend to be good at what you're interested in, and vice versa.<br /> <br /> But I do kind of get what you're going for. Have you ever encountered something it just feels right to you? Before the encounter, you didn't even know that it existed. But once you found it, it just clicked. It's as if it's something that you always wanted, but never knew that you wanted. <i>That's</i> the Yearning.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-05T16:50:37-08:00","id":1471877,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1471534-sorcery-a-surrogate-for-the-yearning-is-possible?p=1471850#post1471850\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure about this sorcery as talent analogy. I've always read it as a desire to understand esoteric mysteries. On the other hand, I am familiar with the notion that you tend to be good at what you're interested in, and vice versa.<br /> <br /> But I do kind of get what you're going for. Have you ever encountered something it just feels right to you? Before the encounter, you didn't even know that it existed. But once you found it, it just clicked. It's as if it's something that you always wanted, but never knew that you wanted. <i>That's</i> the Yearning.</div> </div> </div> </div>Kind of that.<br /> <br /> As I said earlier, <i>talent</i> is itself an ill defined thing. Or, better said, a common word for all the things that make a person be better than most at something, but people can't point to what it is.<br /> <br /> In Psychology we study a lot of those things, and so I can say that, in very, <i>very</i> crude terms, the <i>Yearning</i> is how true talent really looks like most of the time. Even the fact that it may not be exactly the same thing for everyone, but the end result is pretty much the same phenomena.<br /> <br /> How many people that have a talent for something don't also love that? They may not love that more than their goals, but they love that nonetheless. And those that don't, but still have this talent and develop the skill, even if they don't call it love, given how much they dedicate to it in practice, can you really say they don't? And those that chose to not develop what they have a talent for, can you really say they had a talent if you never saw them going further?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-05T18:19:51-08:00","id":1471891,"title":"","content":"Yeah; I do get what you're saying. My only problem with it is that \u2014 how do I put this? \u2014 the average gamer, who isn't as well versed as you are on psychology, is likely to misunderstand what you're saying. That is, my issue isn't with what you're trying to say, but with how your trying to phrase it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-05T18:44:02-08:00","id":1471893,"title":"","content":"Well, this is something I have to live with. I really have a hard time getting how other people will understand what I say, sometimes. And sometimes the right way to phrase it doesn't make any sense to me at all, but it works. Comes with being autistic, having formal knowledge exacerbates it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}